Array
An array in Java is a container object that holds a fixed number of values of the same type.
Each value in the array is called an element, and every element is accessed using an index.

Key points:
    Arrays store multiple values in a single variable
    All elements in an array must be of the same data type
    Array size is fixed once created
    Elements are stored in contiguous memory locations

    Why Do We Use Arrays?
        Arrays help us manage multiple related values efficiently.

        For example,
            imagine storing the marks of 100 students.
            Without arrays, you would need 100 variables.

        With arrays:
            int[] marks = new int[100];
            You can store all 100 marks in one array.

    Advantages of arrays:
        1. Store multiple values using one variable.
        2. Reduce unnecessary variable declarations.
        3. Access elements using indexes.
        4. Easily process data using loops.
        5. Make programs more organized.

    Real-world examples:
        - Student marks
        - Employee salaries
        - Product prices
        - Daily temperatures
        - Monthly sales records

Array Declaration and Initialization

    dataType[] arrayName;

        Example:
            int[] numbers;
            double[] prices;
            String[] names;
            char[] letters;
        also written like this following
            int numbers[];

Array initialization
        int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};


        Here:
        - int is the data type.
        - numbers is the array name.
        - {10, 20, 30, 40, 50} contains the elements.

Declaration and initialization separately

            int[] numbers;
            numbers = new int[]{10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

        You can also declare and initialize an array in one statement:
            int[] numbers = new int[]{10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

Creating Arrays Using new
        Java provides the new keyword to create arrays dynamically.

        Syntax:
            dataType[] arrayName = new dataType[size];


        Example:
            int[] numbers = new int[5];


        This creates an integer array with five elements.

        Initially, its elements contain default values:
        Index:    0   1   2   3   4
        Value:    0   0   0   0   0


        You can assign values later:
            numbers[0] = 10;
            numbers[1] = 20;
            numbers[2] = 30;
            numbers[3] = 40;
            numbers[4] = 50;

Array Indexing
    Every element in an array has a unique position called an index.
    Java array indexing starts from 0, not 1.

    Example:
        int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

   Last index = Array length - 1

Accessing and Updating Array Elements

    You can access an element using its index.

A. Accessing elements
    int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

        System.out.println(numbers[0]);
        System.out.println(numbers[2]);
        System.out.println(numbers[4]);

Updating elements
    You can change an element by assigning a new value to its index.
        int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

        numbers[2] = 100;

        System.out.println(numbers[2]);

The length Property
    Java provides a built-in length property to find the size of an array.

    Example:
        int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        System.out.println(numbers.length);

            length vs length()
        Expression	        Used for
        array.length	    Array size
        string.length()	    String length

Traversing Arrays Using Loops
    Traversing an array means visiting each element one by one.
    You can use different loops to traverse an array.

A. Using a for loop

        public static void main(String[] args) {

            int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

            for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
                System.out.println(numbers[i]);
            }
        }

B. Using an enhanced for loop
    Also called a for-each loop.

        int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

        for (int num : numbers) {
            System.out.println(num);
        }

Common Array Errors
        Beginners often make mistakes while working with arrays.
        Let's understand the most common ones.

        A. ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
        This exception occurs when you try to access an invalid array index.
            int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30};

            System.out.println(numbers[5]);

        Why?
        The array contains only three elements.
        Valid indexes are: 0,1,2

        B. Using an incorrect loop condition
            int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30};

            for (int i = 0; i <= numbers.length; i++) {
                System.out.println(numbers[i]);
            }

            Why is it wrong?
            The last valid index is numbers.length - 1.
            Using <= makes the loop try to access an invalid index. use <

        C. NullPointerException
            This occurs when you try to access an array reference that is null.
            int[] numbers = null;

            System.out.println(numbers[0]);

            Solution:
            int[] numbers = new int[5];
            System.out.println(numbers[0]);

        D. Negative array size
            You cannot create an array with a negative size.
            int[] numbers = new int[-5];

        E. Accessing an array before initialization
            int[] numbers;